1. pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py:1810 — 'recursiveFlag' is only an options-dict key checked via `options.get('recursiveFlag', True)`; it is a deprecated backward-compatibility flag that wraps a user-supplied substrateFun (legacy hack to keep recursiveFlag=False supported). It is not a string used for recursion depth parsing, allocation sizing, or an uncontrolled loop bound. No attacker-controlled resource amplification. False positive (the string-literal heuristic just matched a name containing "recursive").

2. pyasn1/codec/ber/encoder.py:198, 240, 838 — 'maxChunkSize' is a caller-supplied encoding tunable documented in the encoder options (#: maxChunkSize: :py:class:`int`, line 916) and set internally by the CER encoder to the protocol-mandated value 1000 (pyasn1/codec/cer/encoder.py:96). It bounds chunk sizes for CER chunking of BIT STRING / OCTET STRING; callers choose it, and a caller setting an absurd value only affects their own encoding call. There is no untrusted-input path converting attacker data into a loop bound or allocation size. False positives from the DoS string heuristics.

No recursion-depth guard or limit exists that is missing; the actual recursive-descent BER decoder uses an explicit state machine (`asn1Spec`/`state` loop with stDecodeValue/stTryAsExplicitTag) rather than unbounded Python recursion, and the only explicit recursion-break observed (univ.py:876) is a correct anti-recursion fix in BitString.prettyPrint. No genuine resource-exhaustion (CWE-400/664) vulnerability confirmed; the heuristics are pattern-matching on identifier names only.
